 The wave dynamics, wave-induced forces on the pier, and effects of wave energy on the pier structure
Abstract: This report presents an analysis of the wave dynamics, wave-induced forces, and effects of wave energy on a stone pier located on a coastal area. The pier is subjected to waves from different directions with varying wave heights and periods. This report provides an overview of the wave dynamics, wave-induced forces, and effects of wave energy on the pier structure. The results from the analysis are presented in the form of tables, graphs, and charts. The analysis is based on the data collected from a wave gage and a wave-induced force meter installed on the pier. The results show that the wave-induced forces on the pier are significant, with the largest force occurring when the waves are coming from the southwest direction. The wave energy on the pier is also significant, with the largest energy occurring when the waves are coming from the southwest direction. The results from this analysis can be used to design and install a new pier that is more suitable for the coastal area in question. Additionally, the results can be used to design and install a new wave energy converter that can harness the wave energy and convert it into electricity. This report provides valuable information for engineers and researchers who are interested in wave dynamics, wave-induced forces, and wave energy on a stone pier. It also provides valuable information for coastal engineers who are interested in designing and installing new coastal structures like piers and wave energy converters.

1. Introduction:
Coastal areas are known for their beautiful scenery and are a popular destination for tourists. However, they also pose a significant threat to the structural integrity of buildings and structures that are built along the coast. Waves and tides can cause damage to piers, bridges, and other coastal structures, and this damage can be costly and even dangerous to humans.

